s become until this month the biden  campaign and put a coronavirus related  pause on door knocking  while the trump campaign had not they're  very proud of their ground game they say  they've been knocking on doors long  before you guys started doing this  i can't tell you what they've been doing  i can tell you we've been taking this  pandemic seriously  the biden campaign also seriously knows  it needs to do better here than hillary  clinton did four years earlier  losing by the thinnest margin of any  state less than 11  000 votes so what's your theory of what  happened in 2016  why did turnout fall from 2012 for the  democrats  michiganders did not come out and vote i  mean it was a historically low election  yeah donald trump won with a with a  minority he only got 47  or so we don't have tidal waves here in  michigan but i do think that this  year is going to dwarf anything we've  ever seen and i think that bodes well  for  joe biden and i think it bodes well for  michigan donald trump has been  not knocking on doors but at his rallies  bringing up the suburbs all the time  yeah we saved suburbia in the u.s  he says he saved the suburbs suburbs  like these  these suburbs didn't need to be safe  though and the people who call these  suburbs home  cares much about equity care as much  about  ensuring every child has a path to a  good education they care  about every family being able to drink  their water and about climate change so  those those dog whistles  don't really land when you're really  talking to voters here  40 minutes away in detroit michigan's  republican chairwoman laura cox says a  few gubernatorial door knocks this late  in the election  is no match for the gop's turnout  operation the campaign claims to have  knocked on a million doors in michigan  alone  we're in a great place we're going to  win again for the president the vice  president  the enthusiasm is palpable  but we noticed a different kind of  enthusiasm scrawled across the wall of 